---
import TutorialLayout from '../../../layouts/TutorialLayout.astro';
import CompileWidget from '../../../components/tutorials/CompileWidget';
---

<TutorialLayout
  title="FMU Export for MATLAB"
  breadcrumbs={[
    { label: 'Tutorials', href: '/tutorials' },
    { label: 'Code Generation Workflows', href: '/tutorials' },
    { label: 'FMU Export' },
  ]}
  prev={{ label: 'Custom Templates', href: '/tutorials/workflows/custom-templates' }}
  next={{ label: 'PID DC Motor', href: '/tutorials/controls/dc-motor-pid' }}
>
  <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold text-text mb-4">What is an FMU?</h2>
  <p class="text-text-muted leading-relaxed mb-4">
    A Functional Mock-up Unit (FMU) is a standardized container for simulation models defined by the
    <a href="https://fmi-standard.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-teal hover:text-accent underline">Functional Mock-up Interface (FMI)</a>
    standard. An FMU packages a model's compiled code, its description (XML), and any required resources
    into a single <code class="font-mono text-sm text-accent">.fmu</code> zip archive. This makes it
    possible to exchange dynamic models between tools -- MATLAB/Simulink, Dymola, OpenModelica,
    Simulation&shy;X, and many others -- without exposing source code or requiring a shared runtime.
  </p>

  <p class="text-text-muted leading-relaxed mb-6">
    Rumoca supports exporting FMI 2.0-compliant FMUs for co-simulation, meaning your Modelica models
    can be integrated into larger system simulations alongside components from entirely different tools.
  </p>

  <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold text-text mt-8 mb-4">Define Your Model</h2>
  <p class="text-text-muted leading-relaxed mb-4">
    Start with a standard Modelica model. The mass-spring-damper below is a common benchmark. Compile
    it in the widget to inspect the generated code, then follow the CLI steps to produce an FMU.
  </p>

  <CompileWidget
    client:only="react"
    initialSource={`model MassSpringDamper
  Real x(start = 1.0) "Displacement (m)";
  Real v(start = 0.0) "Velocity (m/s)";
  input Real F "External force (N)";
  parameter Real m = 1.0 "Mass (kg)";
  parameter Real k = 10.0 "Spring stiffness (N/m)";
  parameter Real c = 0.5 "Damping coefficient (N*s/m)";
equation
  der(x) = v;
  m * der(v) = F - k * x - c * v;
end MassSpringDamper;`}
    modelName="MassSpringDamper"
  />

  <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold text-text mt-12 mb-4">Export as FMU via CLI</h2>
  <p class="text-text-muted leading-relaxed mb-4">
    Use the <code class="font-mono text-sm text-accent">rumoca export-fmu</code> command to generate
    an FMI 2.0 co-simulation FMU from your Modelica source file.
  </p>

  <div class="p-4 rounded-lg bg-surface border border-border font-mono text-sm text-text-muted mb-6">
    <pre class="overflow-x-auto"><code>{`# Export an FMI 2.0 co-simulation FMU
rumoca export-fmu model.mo --output model.fmu

# Verify the contents of the generated archive
unzip -l model.fmu`}</code></pre>
  </div>

  <p class="text-text-muted leading-relaxed mb-6">
    The resulting <code class="font-mono text-sm text-accent">model.fmu</code> contains a
    <code class="font-mono text-sm text-accent">modelDescription.xml</code> that declares all
    variables, parameters, and causality annotations, along with the compiled shared library for your
    platform.
  </p>

  <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold text-text mt-8 mb-4">Import into MATLAB / Simulink</h2>
  <p class="text-text-muted leading-relaxed mb-4">
    MATLAB can load FMUs through the
    <a href="https://www.mathworks.com/matlabcentral/fileexchange/64118-fmi-kit-for-simulink" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-teal hover:text-accent underline">FMI Kit for Simulink</a>
    or the built-in FMU import block (R2017b+). Below is a minimal MATLAB script that loads and
    simulates the exported FMU.
  </p>

  <div class="p-4 rounded-lg bg-surface border border-border font-mono text-sm text-text-muted mb-6">
    <pre class="overflow-x-auto"><code>{`% Load the FMU using FMI Kit
fmu = fmikit.loadFMU('model.fmu');

% Set parameter overrides
fmu.setParameter('k', 20.0);
fmu.setParameter('c', 1.0);

% Simulate for 10 seconds
result = fmu.simulate('StopTime', 10);

% Plot displacement
plot(result.time, result.values('x'));
xlabel('Time (s)');
ylabel('Displacement (m)');
title('Mass-Spring-Damper Response');
grid on;`}</code></pre>
  </div>

  <p class="text-text-muted leading-relaxed mb-4">
    In Simulink, you can also drag an FMU block into your diagram and point it at the
    <code class="font-mono text-sm text-accent">.fmu</code> file. Simulink automatically reads
    the model description, exposes the input/output ports, and handles the co-simulation stepping.
  </p>

  <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold text-text mt-8 mb-4">Co-Simulation Use Cases</h2>
  <p class="text-text-muted leading-relaxed mb-4">
    FMI 2.0 co-simulation is particularly useful when you need to couple models from different
    domains or tools into a single system simulation:
  </p>
  <ul class="list-disc list-inside text-text-muted leading-relaxed mb-6 space-y-1">
    <li>Connecting a Rumoca plant model with a Simulink controller for hardware-in-the-loop testing</li>
    <li>Embedding a thermal model alongside a mechanical model in a multi-physics simulation</li>
    <li>Sharing validated models with collaborators who use different simulation environments</li>
    <li>Packaging models for deployment in real-time simulation platforms</li>
  </ul>

  <p class="text-text-muted leading-relaxed">
    Because FMI is a vendor-neutral standard, your exported FMU works with any compliant tool --
    no lock-in to a single simulation platform.
  </p>
</TutorialLayout>
